`check conform` (#703) runs one zk2 service against the contract revision its
descriptor claims, as a suite: one verdict per case and resource, the finding on
the yes. A verdict verb: 0 every case asked passed, 1 a violation, 2 no
verdict — and every failure before the run is that 2.

```console
$ zenctl check conform --help
Run one service against the contract revision it claims, as a suite

zk2's conformance suite (#703): the service at SYSTEM/SERVICE, against
the revision of IFACE its descriptor claims (spec §3.3), read through
a session in the deployment's namespace. Every case is one verdict,
on one resource:

  contract-served  the bundle the descriptor names is served and
                   verifies (§8.4)
  resource-served  each exposed stream, state and event resource is
                   heard in the --for window, or answers a GET (§8.2)
  payload-type     every sample and reply decodes as its declared type
                   and satisfies it (§7.2, §7.3)
  qos              every sample rode the declared QoS (§2.4)
  operation        a call draws a value on its own key or a valid
                   envelope, never silence (O3); one summary per
                   replier (O6); a response of its type
  fanout-refused   a call over a template's wildcard to an operation
                   that forbids fan-out is refused fanout_forbidden
                   (O2)
  state-stamp      every state mutation and reply carries the owner's
                   own stamp, its clock the descriptor's meta.zid,
                   compared by value (S1)
  state-get        the owner answers a GET, each reply stamped (S2)
  freshness        each member of a stream or state resource that
                   declares freshness.ttl_s is confirmed within it at
                   the end of the window: by this run's receive
                   clock, or a reply's stamp against a clock trusted
                   to the HLC delta (freshness.v1); a resource with no
                   horizon is not asked
  budget           each templated state holds no more live members
                   than its bound, the descriptor's lowered
                   cardinality else the contract's (§2.7), by the
                   owner's GET: more is the finding, within it a pass
                   only when the GET ran to its final reply; no
                   ceiling and an operation are not asked
  health           the service, when its descriptor lists health.v1,
                   is healthy: unhealthy or stale is the finding
                   (health.v1 §5)
  health-aggregation  its status agrees with its checks: a check
                   worse than a fresh status in both readings is the
                   finding (health.v1 §2.2)
  rate             an event's occurrences per member within its rate
                   (§2.7): n + 1 less than the period apart is the
                   finding in any window; kept is a pass only over a
                   window of a whole period (a minute for low, an
                   hour for rare and burst) that lost no delivery,
                   the owner present throughout
  budget-window    each templated stream or event holds no more live
                   members than its bound, over the window (§2.7): a
                   stream's heard within an hour, an event's within
                   its retention; more is the finding in any window,
                   within it a pass only over a window of at least
                   that span (--for), lost no delivery, the owner
                   present throughout
  config-projection  when its descriptor lists config.v1, its status
                   is its view's projection: a mismatch as the window
                   opens and at its end is the finding (config.v1
                   §2.19)
  config-fresh     its view and its status are each confirmed within
                   60 s at the window's end, never deleted (§2.3)
  config-view      its view is well-formed: its own address and
                   instance, names unique, bounds ordered, no
                   sensitive value carried, no pending change past
                   its deadline by its own clock (§2.2, §2.15)
  config-refusal   each refusal checkable without changing anything,
                   one subject per probe: a set on an undeclared
                   group not_found, on a group outside the allowlist
                   unavailable (config), over the wildcard
                   fanout_forbidden; confirm, cancel and extend with
                   a token naming nothing not_found; persist listed
                   unavailable answered so (§2.5, §2.10, §2.13). Each
                   probe is a dry run against a revision no owner
                   holds; one a broken owner could act on (a control
                   while a change is pending, persist with a last
                   change) only under --i-know
  config-dry-run   a dry run of a hot group's running values answers
                   applied, the revision and the changes as they
                   were (§2.9)
  alarm-key        when its descriptor lists alarms.v1, every alarm
                   key it holds re-derives from its document: a key
                   that is not its document's is the finding, one
                   only through the sanitizing step a pass naming
                   §2.3's SHOULD (alarms.v1 §2.2, §2.3)
  alarm-document   every alarm document it puts is an owner's: strict
                   JSON of the schema, its times in range,
                   observed_at_ms not before timestamp nor on a
                   resolved document (alarms.v1 §2.4)

"Lost no delivery" is established, never assumed (§2.7, 0.25): this
run lost nothing, and the resource is reliable with block, which no
router queue drops. A best-effort or drop resource's window never
is, whatever its length, and its case names the QoS. A window's span
counts from its subscription plus --timeout, and the owner is
watched --lease and --timeout past its end, where a window could
pass. Over the same span this run watches itself (0.27): a transport
of its own session that closed, or a stall of its process longer
than --wait-before-close (or --lease less one keep-alive), means a
router may have given up on it, and no window is lossless.

A reply's stamp is aged only against a clock this run measured on a
live put of the same clock, or on --clocks-synced; otherwise its age
is unobservable, never fresh. A stale member is a finding about the
value, never about the owner's presence.

Only operations declared idempotent are called — with a request
synthesized from the bundle — unless --i-know: every other call is a
write. A resource silent in the window is unobservable, never a pass
and never unserved; an operation silent while its service holds its
tokens is a finding (O3), its message naming the access control that
returns empty too (O5).

Exit 0 every case asked passed, 1 a violation, 2 no verdict: a case
left unobservable, the service not visible, or the run could not
start. A case this run cannot judge here — budget-window over a
window shorter than an hour, or of a best-effort stream — is
--skip'ped rather than left to read 2: not asked neither passes nor
fails. --junit FILE also writes
the suite as JUnit XML: a failure per violation, an error per
unobservable case, skipped per case not asked.

Usage: zenctl check conform [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`: one service, since a suite judges one owner

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one): the revision under test is the one the service's descriptor claims, and a fingerprint given here must be it

Options:
      --for <SECS>
          How long the stream, state and event resources are listened to, seconds
          
          [default: 5]

      --i-know
          Call every exposed operation, not only those declared idempotent: each call is a write, with a synthesized request

      --junit <FILE>
          Also write the suite as JUnit XML to FILE

      --seed <SEED>
          The seed requests are synthesized with — same seed, same requests
          
          [default: 42]

      --trust-admin-space
          Trust every admin-space answer, not only a verified router's: an owner's own stamp passes `state-stamp` only against the routers this run verified (spec §4.2, 0.17). Pass it only when the deployment's grants deny `@/**` queryables to every principal (§11.1)

      --calls-granted
          This tool's grants let it call the service, or the deployment runs no access control: a silent call to a present service is then a finding (O3). No tool can observe its grants (spec §5.1, 0.17), so without this flag such a silence is unobservable

      --clocks-synced
          This host's clock and the owners' agree within the HLC delta (500 ms), as the deployment keeps them: a state reply's stamp is then aged against this clock (freshness.v1 §2.6). Without it, a stamp is aged only against a clock this run measured on a live put

      --lease <SECS>
          The deployment's link lease, seconds (zenoh's `transport/link/tx/lease`): a path gone silent withdraws the owner's token only when it runs out, so a window that could pass is followed by this, and --timeout, of watching the owner (spec §2.7, 0.25)
          
          [default: 10]

      --wait-before-close <SECS>
          The routers' wait_before_close, seconds (zenoh's `transport/link/tx/queue/congestion_control/block/wait_before_close`): a router waits that long on this run's full queue, then closes the transport. A window is lossless only if this run never stalled longer than it, nor than --lease less one keep-alive (spec §2.7, 0.27)
          
          [default: 5]

      --skip <CASE>
          Do not ask this case (repeatable): every row of it reads `not_asked`, which neither passes nor fails the run

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```

A suite reads one service: an address with a `*` is refused, as an interface
that is not one is — clap's refusals, exit 2.

```console
$ zenctl check conform '*/tc' tc.netif.v1
? 2
error: invalid value '*/tc' for '<SYSTEM/SERVICE>': system "*" is not a plain chunk ([a-z0-9]([a-z0-9._-]*[a-z0-9])?)

For more information, try '--help'.

```

```console
$ zenctl check conform host-a/tc tc-netif
? 2
error: invalid value 'tc-netif' for '<IFACE[@FP]>': interface "tc-netif" is not <name>.v<major> with name segments [a-z][a-z0-9_]* (e.g. `tc.netif.v1`)

For more information, try '--help'.

```

`--skip` takes a case of the suite's closed vocabulary (#735); anything else
is clap's refusal, which lists them.

```console
$ zenctl check conform host-a/tc tc.netif.v1 --skip budgets
? 2
error: invalid value 'budgets' for '--skip <CASE>': not a case id; one of: contract-served, resource-served, payload-type, qos, operation, fanout-refused, state-stamp, state-get, freshness, budget, health, health-aggregation, rate, budget-window, config-projection, config-fresh, config-view, config-refusal, config-dry-run, alarm-key, alarm-document

For more information, try '--help'.

```

A window of no time is refused before anything opens, and a bus that will not
open is the reserved 2: no violation is claimed about a service nobody read.

```console
$ zenctl check conform host-a/tc tc.netif.v1 --for 0
? 2
Error: --for must be a positive number of seconds, got 0
check conform: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

```console
$ zenctl check conform host-a/tc tc.netif.v1 --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
check conform: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```
